    },
    {
      "id": "apparatus",
      "html": "<h4>Apparatus criticus</h4><ul><li><span><span>Text of Agnello</span></span>; </li><li><span class=\"line 1\">line.1: <span>ἐντάδε sic AE 1933.25</span></span><br></li><li><span class=\"line 8\">line.8: <span>ΛΥΓΗΣ sic AE 1933.25</span></span></li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "id": "translation",
      "html": "<h2>English translation</h2><p class=\"translation-author\"><a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5187-5163\">Marta Fogagnolo</a></p>  <p>Here lies the blessed Apra, daughter of Kentianos and Asklepiodote, a good servant of Christ, a tree well planted in the faith, torn away from her brief life and from the affections of this world, leaving sorrow to her own. She returned the loan of her soul, dying on the Kalends of April during the fifteenth consulship of Theodosius and the fourth consulship of Valentinian.</p>  "
    },
    {
      "id": "translation",
      "html": "<h2>Italian translation</h2><p class=\"translation-author\">  </p><p>Qui giace la beata Apra, figlia di Quinziano e di Asclepiodote, bouna serva di Cristo, albero ben piantato nella fede, dipartitasi dalla vita terrena e dagli affetti del mondo, lasciando dolore tra i suoi. Restitui il prestito della vita morendo il 1 aprile sotto il quindicesimo consolato di Teodosio ed il quarto di Valentiniano [trad. di Agnello]</p>  "
    },
    {
      "id": "commentary",
      "html": "<h2>commentary</h2>  <p>The epitaph commemorates the young deceased Apra, daughter of Kentianos and Asklepiodote. For the name Apra, which may represent a variant of Apronia or Aphronia. The father's name is partially restored by Libertini (1931, 40), who also proposed alternative readings, including Sentianus, Gentianus, Ventianus, and Mintianus. The deceased is described as <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">δούλη Χ(ριστο)ῦ ἀγαθή</span> (ll. 3–4; cf. IG XIV 442 = <a href=\"https://sicily.classics.ox.ac.uk/inscription/ISic001267\" target=\"_blank\">ISic001267</a>) and, through a poetic image, as <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">δένδρο[ν καλῶς] | ἐν πίστει ἐφυτευμ[ένον</span> (ll. 4–5), an expression that echoes a well-established image in Christian theology (cf. [Athan.] 28.712.45). The brevity of her life is conveyed by the expression <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">τῇ ὀλιγοχρονίᾳ</span> (l. 6) and by the reference to <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">τοῖς ἰδίοις</span> (l. 9), from whose affection she was torn away. The mention of her parents, together with the absence of any reference to a husband, led Libertini (1931, 41) to suggest that she died a virgin. At l. 10, the expression <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">τῆς ψυχῆς δάνος</span> refers to the soul as a loan that is returned to God after death. Finally, ll. 10–13 conclude the epitaph with the date of the deceased's death, which occurred on the Kalends of April during the fifteenth consulship of Theodosius and the fourth consulship of Valentinian. This internal dating places the inscription in AD 435.</p>  "
    },
    {
      "id": "bibliography",
      "html": "<h2>bibliography</h2>  <ul><li> Libertini 1931 40 n.1 tav. A.1 <a href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10001870\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10001870</a></li><li> 1933 25 <a href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10001283\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10001283</a></li><li> Agnello 1953 no.96 <a href=\"https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002211\" target=\"_blank\">https://biblio.inscriptiones.org/epig10002211</a></li></ul>  <ul><li></li></ul>  "
    },
